<</Title(6/11/2026)/Dest[10 0 R/Fit]/Parent 826 0 R/Prev 946 0 R/Next 947 0 R>>
endobj

1018 0 obj
<</Title(Previous and current readings are inputs used to calculate consumption. Household number identifies the consumer and category identifies the consumer class. Units, energy charge, fixed charge and total are outputs. The tariff limits and rates remain unchanged during one run, so they are constants. Readings and units are whole numbers. Money is represented in paise to avoid decimal rounding. The bill must never be negative and total must equal energy charge plus fixed charge. If readings are stored as text, converting and comparing d digits takes O\(d\) time and d+1 bytes per string; an integer comparison is O\(1\) and uses sizeof\(int\) bytes.)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 949 0 R>>
endobj

1019 0 obj
<</Title(Procedure / Algorithm)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 687 0 R/Prev 949 0 R/First 1090 0 R/Last 1091 0 R/Count -5/Next 1020 0 R>>
endobj

1020 0 obj
<</Title(Program / Source Code)/Dest[11 0 R/Fit]/Parent 687 0 R/Prev 1019 0 R/Next 950 0 R>>
endobj

1021 0 obj
<</Title(To select data types based on range and precision, and calculate consumption correctly when the meter register rolls over.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 953 0 R>>
endobj

1022 0 obj
<</Title(Concept / Theory)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 753 0 R/Prev 953 0 R/First 1092 0 R/Last 1092 0 R/Count -1/Next 1093 0 R>>
endobj

1023 0 obj
<</Title(Output Screenshot)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 753 0 R/Prev 1094 0 R/Next 954 0 R>>
endobj

1024 0 obj
<</Title(For 1200 to 1530, consumption is 330. For 65400 to 120, the difference wraps modulo 65536: 120 \212 65400 = \21265280; modulo 65536 gives 256. It is not negative because conversion to the unsigned 16-bit type wraps into the range 0\20565535. A 16-bit unsigned register changes from 65535 to 0. Integer paise additions remain exact; float additions can accumulate approximation error.)/Dest[12 0 R/Fit]/Parent 954 0 R>>
endobj

